.TH XPROXY\-SIMULATE 8 "" "Xproxy" "System administration"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
.PP
xproxy\-simulate \- what a configuration would decide, and what a change to it would decide differently
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBxproxy\-simulate\fR \fB\-offline\fR \fB\-a\fR \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-b\fR \fIFILE\fR] (\fB\-requests\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-frames\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-pcap\fR \fIFILE\fR \fB\-listener\fR \fINAME\fR) [\fB\-listener\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-json\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBxproxy\-simulate\fR answers one question about a configuration change: what would it decide differently. Given one configuration it reports the decision for each piece of traffic it is handed. Given two it reports only what moved between them, and exits non\-zero when anything did, so a change can be gated on it in review or in a pipeline.
.PP
It is not a linter and it does not reason about the rules. It starts the engine \[u2014] this configuration, in this process, offline \[u2014] and sends the traffic through it, so the answer comes from the same code that would decide it in production: the same WAF profiles and rule files, the same route matching, the same protocol policies, the same filters. A rule that matches here matches there.
.PP
Every listener kind is linked into this program, so it answers about a configuration file naming any role\(aqs listeners without the operator having to work out which of \fBxproxy\fR(8), \fBxgate\fR(8), \fBxrelay\fR(8) or \fBxot\fR(8) would have served it. It opens no management socket and needs no running daemon.
.PP
Nothing reaches a real upstream. Every pool is pointed at a sink inside this process, keeping the pool names and the per\-route assignments \[u2014] which pool a request goes to is part of the policy \[u2014] and changing only where that pool is. A TLS listener is given a throwaway certificate; the estate\(aqs private keys are not read. Every section that reaches outside the machine is switched off, and the output names each one that was, so the answer is never quietly narrower than it looks.
.PP
Nothing is written outside the simulation\(aqs own directory. The state a decision depends on \[u2014] the ban store, the access ledger, the asset and API inventories \[u2014] is copied into it, so the run starts from what the estate has: a banned address stays banned, a grant still approves, a device already in the inventory is not a new device. What a run produces \[u2014] a learning report, a session recording \[u2014] is redirected into it. Those change no decision, which is why they are easy to overlook; a learning report overwritten with a simulation\(aqs traffic would be the worst of them, since those get promoted into policies.
.SH "WHY \-offline IS REQUIRED"
.PP
Neutralising a configuration is not the same as making it inert. The policy itself runs: the filters, any WebAssembly modules, the rule files and the secrets provider are loaded exactly as the daemon would load them. That is the point \[u2014] a simulation of something other than the real policy answers the wrong question \[u2014] and it is also a decision about this machine that belongs to the operator rather than to this program. \fB\-offline\fR is that assertion. Without it nothing is started and nothing is read.
